  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Investigate the low-risk output path first. The current printer inserts each character separately and uses std::endl for every permutation, forcing a flush per line. Compare removing per-line flushes with '\n' against a bounded reusable output buffer flushed in large chunks (and, if useful, disabling iostream/stdio synchronization). Preserve the exact brackets, spaces, line order, and newline behavior; do not buffer the entire output without a memory bound. Measure end-to-end, including output.",
        "B": "Independently examine the enumeration kernel. Compare the existing custom next-permutation routine with a standard-library implementation, and check whether avoidable copies or per-permutation work can be removed without changing the sorted sequence of distinct multiset permutations. Keep the initial ordering and repeated-symbol behavior correct for arbitrary inputs. Treat traversal changes as a separate candidate from output changes so their effects can be measured independently.",
        "C": "Explore whether parallel generation can help on the 4-core ARM64 target. A possible design is to divide the lexicographically ordered permutation space into disjoint prefix ranges, with workers generating distinct permutations and an ordered, bounded handoff to output. Preserve global lexicographic order and avoid unbounded buffering. Because emitting every permutation may dominate runtime, pursue this only if a complete end-to-end measurement shows a gain; otherwise report that the added complexity is not worthwhile.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidate programs from A, B, and C that retain correctness on the full validation set. Compare measured variants independently, then measure any proposed combination: improvements may not add, and integration can regress performance. Preserve the original input handling and exact output contract for arbitrary strings, including repeated symbols and whitespace removal. Prefer the simplest measured winner; do not specialize to observed test values or change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
        "analysis": "The task is Problem D: read a whitespace-insensitive string and emit every distinct permutation in lexicographic order, with the specified bracket-and-space format. The source sorts once and advances with a custom next-permutation routine; its clearest low-risk opportunity is per-character stream insertion plus std::endl flushing every output line. Output volume is unavoidable, so all candidates must preserve the complete output. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.13166569576909146 seconds and a narrow range of three timings; there are no measured variants or per-case timings, so prioritize low-risk experiments and require end-to-end evidence, especially for parallelism."
      },
      "request_sha256": "1b7a986f0d16c5d97c5958e1c9aa903b52ed2b3226698d4de7e68524fa3fb150",
